::GEO_U06_L02_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.9<br><br>Question:<br>What is the equation of a circle with center (-3, 4) and radius 6? {
=A. (x + 3)&sup2; + (y - 4)&sup2; = 36#The equation uses opposite signs for the center and r&sup2; = 36.
~B. (x - 3)&sup2; + (y + 4)&sup2; = 36#This reverses both center signs.
~C. (x + 3)&sup2; + (y - 4)&sup2; = 6#The right side must be r&sup2;, not r.
~D. (x - 4)&sup2; + (y + 3)&sup2; = 36#This switches the coordinate values.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.10<br><br>Question:<br>What center and radius come from (x - 5)&sup2; + (y + 1)&sup2; = 49? {
~A. Center (-5, 1), radius 7#The radius is correct, but the signs are reversed.
=B. Center (5, -1), radius 7#The center is (5, -1), and the radius is sqrt(49).
~C. Center (5, -1), radius 49#The center is correct, but the radius is 7.
~D. Center (-5, 1), radius 49#Both the signs and radius interpretation are incorrect.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.9<br><br>Question:<br>Which method finds the diameter length from endpoints A(1, 2) and B(7, 2)? {
~A. Use slope only#Slope shows direction, not distance.
~B. Use midpoint only#The midpoint finds the center, not the diameter length.
=C. Use distance formula on the endpoints#The distance between diameter endpoints is the diameter.
~D. Use area of a triangle#Triangle area is not relevant to diameter length.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.9<br><br>Question:<br>A circle has center (0, 0) and passes through (0, 8). What is r&sup2;? {
~A. 0#The radius is not zero because the point is 8 units from the center.
~B. 8#This is r, not r&sup2;.
~C. 16#This would match radius 4, not radius 8.
=D. 64#The radius is 8, so r&sup2; = 64.
}

::GEO_U06_L02_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L02_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CIR.10<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement matches the graph of (x + 2)&sup2; + (y - 3)&sup2; = 16? {
=A. A circle centered at (-2, 3) with radius 4#The center is (-2, 3), and sqrt(16) = 4.
~B. A circle centered at (2, -3) with radius 4#The signs of the center are reversed.
~C. A circle centered at (-2, 3) with radius 16#The radius is 4, not 16.
~D. A line through (-2, 3)#The equation represents a circle, not a line.
}

